<DocsLayout
  title="CAD geometry"
  subtitle="Drawings, sketches, meshes, and an exact boundary representation. All of it is plain data in @cadcamfun/core."
  description="How CAD geometry works: the drawing document, parametric history, mesh Booleans, the B-rep kernel, and technical drawings."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="drawing" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Drawing document</h2>
    <p>
      A drawing is the 2D document stored on a project. <code>DrawingSchema</code> in
      <code>packages/core/src/cad/entities.ts</code> holds a schema version, an id, a name, a
      display unit, layers, entities, and a revision counter. Geometry is always stored in
      millimetres. Angles on arcs are radians. The <code>units</code> field is only how the
      editor labels numbers; it does not rescale the stored points.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every entity is a tagged record. The tag is <code>_tag</code>. The kinds are Line, Circle,
      Arc, Polyline, Rectangle, Ellipse, Spline, Text, Dimension, Hatch, and Block. Shared fields
      are id, layer, visibility, lock, an optional line style, an optional 2D transform, and
      optional user data. Selection is not stored on the entity. It lives in the editor.
    </p>
    <p>
      A line style is a colour (0–255 channels, optional alpha), a width, and a type:
      continuous, dashed, dotted, center, or phantom. A transform is scale, then rotation, then
      translation. Shear uses the affine form in <code>transform.ts</code> instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>entitySchemaOf(tag)</code> returns the Effect schema for one kind, so a toolbar can
      decode a line without casting the whole union. <code>emptyDrawing</code> starts a document
      with one default layer. Older JSON that still uses a <code>type</code> field is upgraded by
      <code>migrate.ts</code> and then decoded; a failure is <code>DrawingMigrationError</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Import">
{`import { cad, drawing, entitySchemaOf } from "@cadcamfun/core"

const lineSchema = entitySchemaOf("Line")
const exact = cad.brep`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>packages/core/src/index.ts</code> re-exports CAD symbols both flat and as the
      <code>cad</code> namespace. CAM and technical drawings are namespaces only
      (<code>cam</code>, <code>drawing</code>), so a name like <code>Path</code> does not collide.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sketch" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Sketch and parameters</h2>
    <p>
      A sketch in <code>sketch.ts</code> is points, lines, circles, and arcs that share point ids.
      A Levenberg–Marquardt solver drives the constraints. The result reports degrees of freedom,
      redundant constraints, and conflicts. <code>sketchProfiles</code> walks the solved geometry
      and returns closed loops. Those loops are what an extrude consumes. A loop inside a loop is
      a hole (even-odd), so a ring is two circles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dimensions in a history are not always numbers. <code>expr.ts</code> evaluates a parameter
      table: arithmetic, unit suffixes (<code>mm</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>deg</code>),
      constants, and functions. <code>resolveParameters</code> orders the table by dependency and
      rejects cycles. <code>dimensionValue</code> is what a feature reads after that pass. By the
      time the sketch solver runs, every dimension is a number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Planes are right-handed. XY has normal +Z, YZ has normal +X, ZX has normal +Y. A sketch can
      also sit on a plane feature created earlier in the history, offset along that plane’s normal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="history" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Feature history</h2>
    <p>
      <code>history.ts</code> is a parameter table plus an ordered feature list.
      <code>rebuild(history)</code> replays the list from the top. Changing <code>width</code>
      re-solves every sketch that mentions it, rebuilds every solid made from those sketches, and
      redoes every Boolean after that. The return value is mesh bodies, the resolved parameters,
      the sketch solve records, sheet-metal flat patterns, and an <code>issues</code> list. A
      feature that cannot be built is reported and skipped. Features that do not depend on it
      still build.
    </p>
    <p>
      Feature kinds in that file include sketch, extrude, revolve, hole, pattern, mirror, sweep,
      loft, shell, fillet, catalogue primitives, coil, move, scale, split, combine, rib, sheet
      metal, thread, text, push-pull, draft, mate, and plane. An extrude combines with the bodies
      already built using <code>new</code>, <code>union</code>, <code>subtract</code>, or
      <code>intersect</code>. A revolve turns a profile about a sketch line or about the plane’s
      own X or Y axis. A hole is a cylinder subtracted at a point on a plane. A pattern copies an
      earlier solid along a line or around Z.
    </p>
    <p>
      Corner and edge treatments (chamfer or fillet of a given size) are applied on the profile
      before the mesh is built. <code>history-edit.ts</code> edits the history immutably: set a
      dimension by path, rename a parameter, reorder or remove a feature, or start from a
      template. The editor keeps the previous value for undo.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>parametric-from-element.ts</code> turns a catalogue element into a history with named
      parameters. Placement stays on the element, not in the parameter table.
      <code>script.ts</code> is a fluent <code>model()</code> builder that produces the same
      <code>History</code> the panel edits. <code>runModelScript</code> evaluates caller-trusted
      JavaScript into that history.
    </p>
    <DocCallout title="Two replays of the same history">
      <p>
        <code>rebuild</code> produces triangle meshes. <code>history-brep.ts</code> replays the
        same features as exact solids where the kernel supports them: plain extrude (including
        draft and fillets), revolve, holes, sweeps, blends, shells, catalogue primitives, and
        exact Booleans. A feature the kernel cannot build stays mesh-only and records the reason.
        It does not silently approximate an exact solid.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="mesh" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Mesh solids</h2>
    <p>
      <code>solid.ts</code> describes catalogue solids (box, cylinder, sphere, cone, torus,
      extrude, revolve) and turns them into a <code>Mesh</code>: <code>Float32Array</code>
      positions and <code>Uint32Array</code> indices. <code>meshOf</code>, bounds, and volume read
      that mesh. The viewport and the mesh Booleans share it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>csg.ts</code> unions, subtracts, and intersects triangle soups with a BSP, then
      <code>cleanPolygonSoup</code> welds the result. The output is correct up to the input mesh
      tolerance. It is not an exact solid. Inputs must be closed and wound consistently.
      <code>mesh-clean.ts</code> welds vertices, repairs T-junctions, merges coplanar faces, and
      re-triangulates. <code>mesh-topology.ts</code> and <code>mesh-normals.ts</code> check and
      repair the mesh after that.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>design.ts</code> is the editor’s design space: right-handed, Z up, XY on the ground.
      Solid rotation in the scene is degrees. Arc and revolution angles stay radians.
      <code>checkModel</code> reports non-finite sizes, missing Boolean operands, scales under the
      drawing tolerance, and parts outside an optional stock box.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="brep" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Exact B-rep</h2>
    <p>
      <code>cad.brep</code> is a boundary-representation kernel: analytic and NURBS geometry, and
      topology stored as plain tables. A body is vertices, edges (curve segments), coedges (which
      side of an edge a loop uses, plus an optional p-curve), loops, faces, and shells. Material
      sits on the left of a loop. The tables serialise to JSON.
    </p>
    <p>
      Curves and surfaces in <code>geom.ts</code> follow STEP parameterisation: line, circle,
      plane, cylinder, cone, sphere, torus, surface of revolution, extrusion, and NURBS.
      <code>primitives.ts</code>, <code>sweep.ts</code>, <code>loft.ts</code>, and
      <code>path-sweep.ts</code> build solids. <code>boolean.ts</code> intersects, imprints,
      classifies, and sews. An invalid Boolean throws <code>BooleanError</code>. It does not
      return a partial solid.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>tessellate.ts</code> turns faces into a watertight welded mesh at a tolerance taken
      from the model diagonal, and that mesh is the same <code>Mesh</code> the viewport uses.
      <code>mass.ts</code> computes mass properties. <code>validate.ts</code> checks the body.
      <code>features.ts</code> recognises holes from coaxial concave cylinders and cones
      (counterbore, countersink, drill point, through) without reading the feature history.
      <code>drawing-edges.ts</code> classifies crease edges against smooth tangent edges for
      hidden-line drawings. <code>budget.ts</code> caps how much work one call may do.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="exchange" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Import and export</h2>
    <p>
      <code>dxf.ts</code> reads and writes LINE, CIRCLE, ARC, LWPOLYLINE (with bulge), ELLIPSE,
      TEXT, and MTEXT. It keeps layers and honours <code>$INSUNITS</code>. The drawing is still
      stored in millimetres. DXF angles are degrees only at that boundary.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>import-drawing.ts</code> turns a DXF, an SVG, or a <code>Drawing</code> into closed
      loops and then into extruded scene elements. Nested loops become holes, subtracted from the
      outer solid. The function is pure and deterministic.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>import-step.ts</code> reads a prismatic part: the footprint is the bottom faces on XY,
      the height is the Z extent, and the result is an extrusion. Geometry that is not a prism is
      counted in warnings, not approximated. An optional up-axis remap runs first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Full solids go through <code>brep/step.ts</code> (AP203, AP214, AP242). Unsupported entities
      are skipped with a warning. <code>scene-step.ts</code> places each scene element the same way
      the mesh placer does (gizmo about the bounding-box centre) and <code>sceneToStep</code>
      writes AP214. A mesh-only solid is faceted and the export says so.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sheets" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Technical drawings</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>drawing</code> namespace builds a sheet from the 3D model.
      <code>project.ts</code> makes orthographic views with hidden-line removal from a mesh or
      from exact edges. Crease, boundary, and silhouette edges are kept; smooth tangent edges can
      be dropped. View space is millimetres, Y up.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>section.ts</code> cuts the mesh with a half-space, caps the cut, projects it, and
      hatches the section loops. A detail view is a circular zoom of an existing view.
      <code>dimensions.ts</code> plans callouts from the rebuilt history (hole groups, threads,
      radii), not by guessing from the mesh. <code>sheet.ts</code> lays views out in first or
      third angle, adds a title block, and returns both a <code>Drawing</code> the 2D editor can
      open and a report of dimensions, callouts, warnings, and whether edges came from the exact
      kernel or the mesh. <code>svg.ts</code> writes that drawing to SVG: millimetre viewBox, Y
      flipped, layer colours, and line types.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="modules" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Modules</h2>
    <p>Everything below is under <code>packages/core/src/cad</code> unless noted.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>entities.ts</code></td><td>Tagged 2D entities and the drawing document.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>vec.ts</code>, <code>arc-math.ts</code>, <code>bounds.ts</code></td><td>Vectors, arc normalisation, and bounding boxes. Millimetres and radians.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>shapes.ts</code>, <code>offset.ts</code>, <code>intersect.ts</code>, <code>snap.ts</code>, <code>spline.ts</code>, <code>tessellate.ts</code></td><td>2D area, offsets, intersections, snapping, splines, and polyline tessellation. CAM profiles reuse the offset.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sketch.ts</code>, <code>expr.ts</code>, <code>sketch-shapes.ts</code></td><td>Constraint solver, parameter expressions, and parametric profiles expanded before the solve.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>history.ts</code>, <code>history-edit.ts</code>, <code>history-brep.ts</code></td><td>Mesh replay, immutable edits, and the exact replay of the same feature list.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>solid.ts</code>, <code>csg.ts</code>, <code>mesh-clean.ts</code></td><td>Catalogue meshes, mesh Booleans, and weld after a Boolean.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>brep/</code></td><td>Exact topology, Booleans, STEP, tessellation, mass, validation, hole recognition, drawing edges.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dxf.ts</code>, <code>import-drawing.ts</code>, <code>import-step.ts</code>, <code>scene-step.ts</code></td><td>DXF round-trip, 2D outline to extrude, prismatic STEP, and scene export to AP214.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sheet-metal.ts</code>, <code>thread.ts</code>, <code>text-solid.ts</code>, <code>fasteners.ts</code>, <code>dfm.ts</code></td><td>Flat patterns, modelled threads, text solids, ISO fastener sizes, and hole sites for machining.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/core/src/drawing</code></td><td>Projection, sections, dimensions, sheet layout, and SVG.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout title="No I/O in this package">
      <p>
        These modules do not open SQLite, the network, or the DOM. The sidecar stores the JSON.
        The viewport draws it. Hot geometry stays plain functions; Effect schemas sit on the
        document boundary.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>
  <DocRelated items={[{ title: "CAM and G-code", href: "/docs/cam" }, { title: "Viewport", href: "/docs/viewport" }, { title: "Setups", href: "/docs/setups" }]} />
</DocsLayout>
